/**
 * @mainpage CFWx — complete repository reference
 *
 * @section intro Purpose and maturity
 * CFWx is a C17 educational numerical/Transformer playground with an analytic
 * CPU training path and an opt-in CUDA reference backend. This manual covers
 * public API contracts, implementation helpers, model owners, tests, experiment
 * drivers, maintenance tooling and design proposals. It is not a production
 * performance, portability or security certification.
 *
 * @section navigation Reading paths
 * - @ref getting_started: local documentation/build commands and navigation.
 * - @ref architecture_guide: repository map and dependency boundaries.
 * - @ref numerical_contracts: ownership, layout, reductions and numerical failures.
 * - @ref training_guide: analytic reverse mode and actual dataset training.
 * - @ref runtime_guide: dropout, batching, checkpoints and fixed/sliding caches.
 * - @ref gpu_guide: unvalidated CUDA implementation and optimization proposals.
 * - @ref testing_guide: correctness tests versus performance claims.
 * - @ref tooling_guide: CMake, Python maintenance, CI, editor and repository metadata.
 * - @ref documentation_guide: how this HTML manual is configured and maintained.
 *
 * @section api API hierarchy
 * Browse @ref cfwx for the complete module tree. Start with @ref core and
 * @ref containers, then @ref maths, @ref models, @ref training and @ref runtime.
 * @ref experiments describes application orchestration; @ref logging describes
 * borrowed logger contexts. @ref gpu is intentionally separate from CPU storage.
 * Files provide inline source, include relations, referenced-by links and optional
 * call graphs. Data Structures list fields and ownership; Modules group symbols
 * by scientific/engineering responsibility rather than source filename alone.
 *
 * @section safety Important constraints
 * Owners begin as zero; no shallow copies. Positive dimensions and caller-owned
 * preallocated outputs are the normal API contract. Failure may partially modify
 * numerical outputs; do not update a model after failed backward. Logging does
 * not make global backend state thread-safe. FNV checksum/fingerprint detects
 * corruption/reproducibility differences, not adversarial tampering. CUDA source
 * documentation is not CUDA execution or validation.
 *
 * @section generation Generate locally
 * @code{.sh}
 * doxygen Doxyfile
 * # Open build-docs/html/index.html in a browser.
 * # Optional CMake integration, no numerical executable is run:
 * cmake -S . -B build -DCFWX_USE_VCPKG=OFF -DCFWX_CUDA=OFF
 * cmake --build build --target docs
 * @endcode
 * Install Doxygen and optionally Graphviz locally. Doxygen parses CUDA as C++
 * source; it never invokes nvcc. Graphviz renders diagrams only.
 */
